// A line discipline: what a terminal does to your keystrokes before a program sees them.
//
// `packages/tty/src/line.wac` is 430 lines and one of the best-evidenced files in the repository —
// every rule read off a real pty, 44 sequences byte for byte, three modes each measured in that
// setting. **None of the rules change here.** What changes is the type the rules answer with, and
// one number the module keeps twice.
//
// Read the README beside this file first; it is where the two findings are argued.

import { Bytes } from "core";
import { Mode } from "std";
import { Buf } from "@/packages/bytes";
import { glyph, columns, rubOut, crlf } from "./render.wac";

/** DEL, the erase character. Not `^H` — only DEL erases, which the pty comparison settled. */
const u8 ERASE = 127;
/** `^U`, kill the whole line. */
const u8 KILL = 21;
/** `^W`, erase the word behind the cursor, and the punctuation behind it. */
const u8 WERASE = 23;
/** `^C`, interrupt. */
const u8 INTR = 3;
/** `^D`, end of file — or end of *line*, when there is something on it. */
const u8 EOFC = 4;
/** `^V`, take the next character literally however it would otherwise be read. */
const u8 LNEXT = 22;
/** `^\`, quit — like `^C` but a different signal, and traditionally the one that dumps core. */
const u8 QUIT = 28;
/** `^R`, reprint: draw the line again, after something scribbled on it. */
const u8 REPRINT = 18;

/**
 * The two signals a terminal can ask for.
 *
 * The original carries an `i32` where `0` means *no signal* and POSIX's numbers mean the other two,
 * and its comment argues the widening it had already done:
 *
 * > **A number rather than the `bool interrupt` this was**, because there are two of them and they
 * > are not the same signal … A flag could only say "one of them happened", so `^\` was delivered as
 * > an ordinary control character.
 *
 * The argument is right and stops one step short. A `bool` holds one of two things and there are
 * three; an `i32` holds four billion and there are three. Both are the wrong size, in opposite
 * directions, and the second one leaves `0` meaning *nothing happened* — a value drawn from the
 * range of the answer, which is the shape this exercise has now found in `Read.code`, `search`'s
 * `NO_MATCH`, `decode`'s `-2` and `Socket`'s negative handle.
 *
 * The POSIX number is a *rendering* of a signal for whoever is delivering it, not the identity of
 * one, so it is a method rather than the representation. `packages/fs/src/proc.wac` has the same
 * two numbers, and the original explains that it deliberately does not import them — *"a line
 * discipline knowing about a process table would be the wrong way round"* — which stays true.
 */
export enum Sig {
  Interrupt,
  Quit,

  /** POSIX's numbering, for a caller that has to put the signal on a wire or in a wait status. */
  i32 number(const this) { return this is Interrupt ? 2 : 3; }
}

/**
 * What one keystroke did to the *program* — as opposed to what it drew, which is `Typed.echo`.
 *
 * **The original is a record of four fields and says why:**
 *
 * > Four things can happen at once — a byte can be echoed, complete a line, and end the input — so
 * > this is a record rather than an enum.
 *
 * One of the four is orthogonal and the other three are not, and the sentence puts them in one
 * group. Reading the ten `return Typed(…)` sites in that file: `line`+`hasLine` is set by four of
 * them, `eof` by one, `signal` by four, `echo` by nine — and **no site sets more than one of the
 * first three.** A newline delivers a line; `^D` on an empty line ends the input; `^C` signals and
 * throws the line away. There is no keystroke that does two of those, because each of them is what
 * happens *instead of* holding on to the line.
 *
 * So the record is right about the echo and wrong about the rest, and the cost is the usual one: a
 * caller must read `hasLine` before `line`, know that `signal == 0` means no signal, and know that
 * `eof` and `hasLine` cannot both be true — three facts the type could have stated and does not.
 */
export enum Effect {
  /** Still typing. */
  Nothing,
  /**
   * A line for the program, **with** its newline — except after `^D`, which delivers without one.
   *
   * Named `Delivered` rather than `Line`, which is what it wants to be called, because `Line` is the
   * struct twelve lines down. `issues/lang/closed/0095` is *a struct named like a variant resolves
   * to the enum* — closed, and the collision it was about is still a collision a reader has to
   * avoid by hand. Worth one line here: the natural name for the arm and the natural name for the
   * machine are the same word, and only one of them can have it.
   */
  Delivered(Bytes bytes),
  /** `^D` on an empty line: the program's read should answer end of input. */
  Eof,
  /** Whatever was being typed is gone. */
  Signal(Sig sig),
}

/**
 * What one keystroke did.
 *
 * `echo` is what to write back to the terminal and it is *always* what to write: a caller that
 * ignores it has a terminal that shows nothing. It is a separate field rather than an arm because it
 * genuinely co-occurs with all four effects — which is the half of the original's argument that
 * holds.
 */
export struct Typed {
  Bytes echo;
  Effect effect;

  Typed nothing()          { return Typed(Bytes.empty(), Nothing); }
  Typed echoed(Bytes echo) { return Typed(echo, Nothing); }
}

/**
 * `termios` as this module uses it: two flags, and the three settings anyone has asked for.
 *
 * The original has three constructors and two `bool` fields on `Line` itself. Pulling them out is
 * what lets a mode be **passed**, which is the gap its README names:
 *
 * > What is missing is anything that *selects* one. `sshd` constructs `Line.create()` and never
 * > changes it, the browser terminal likewise, and there is no `stty` for a program to ask with. So
 * > an editor still cannot have a keystroke at a time — not because the discipline cannot do it, but
 * > because nothing can say so.
 *
 * A `Mode` is a value, so `sshd` can hold the one the session asked for and swap it; and the missing
 * half is one capability call, which the README beside this file argues for.
 *
 * Two flags rather than the forty in `termios`, and named presets rather than a bit-field, because
 * three settings is what has been measured. The original is right that `cbreak` is not `raw`:
 * `stty raw` also clears `ISIG`, which would make `^C` an ordinary byte, and it is not one here.
 */
/*
 * **`Mode` is declared in `std/platform.wac` and this file no longer owns it**, since 2026-09-04.
 *
 * It was here, and the paragraph above is why that looked right: the discipline is what a mode
 * *does*. But the paragraph also names the gap — *"the missing half is one capability call"* — and
 * that call is `In.setMode(Mode)`, on a capability that sits **below** every package. A `Mode`
 * declared here is a type `std` cannot name, so the capability could not be declared at all.
 *
 * So the cause of *nothing can select a mode* was not that nobody wrote the call. It was that the
 * value was one layer too high for the call to be writable, and the layer it was in is the one that
 * needed it first. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
 */

/**
 * The line being typed, and the rules for editing it.
 *
 * Feed it bytes as they arrive from wherever the keyboard is — an ssh `CHANNEL_DATA` payload, a
 * browser's keydown — and it answers what to echo and what, if anything, the program should read.
 */
export struct Line {
  Buf held;
  /**
   * Whether the next byte is taken literally, because the last one was `^V`.
   *
   * The kernel echoes `^` for the `^V`, then a backspace over it, then the literal character's own
   * echo — so the caret appears and is rubbed out as the character arrives. Measured.
   */
  bool literal;
  Mode mode;

  Line create()        { return Line(Buf.create(), false, Mode.canonicalEcho()); }
  Line inMode(Mode m)  { return Line(Buf.create(), false, m); }

  /** Whether anything has been typed on the line so far. */
  bool pending(const this) { return this.held.len > 0; }

  /**
   * The mode this line is in, and the one it was in — so a caller can put it back.
   *
   * Answering the old value is the whole of what `stty` does that a setter does not: an editor that
   * takes a terminal into `cbreak` has to hand it back the way it found it, and a program that
   * guesses *canonical with echo* gets it wrong for anyone who was in a password prompt.
   */
  Mode setMode(this, Mode m) {
    Mode was = this.mode;
    this.mode = m;
    return was;
  }

  /**
   * One byte from the keyboard, through whichever mode this line is in.
   *
   * `canonical` chooses *which* function runs, and `echo` only decides whether what it decided to
   * draw is returned — editing and drawing are separate in `termios` and separate here.
   *
   * One rule is not merely undrawn when the echo goes: **`^R` stops reprinting and becomes a byte in
   * the line**, because reprint *is* an echo and there is nothing to draw again. Measured, and the
   * reason this is a branch rather than a blanked field.
   */
  Typed feed(this, u8 b) {
    if (!this.mode.canonical) { return this.feedByte(b); }
    if (!this.mode.echo && b == REPRINT) {
      this.held.push(b);
      return Typed.nothing();
    }
    Typed t = this.feedLine(b);
    return this.mode.echo ? t : Typed(Bytes.empty(), t.effect);
  }

  /**
   * A run of bytes, as one arrival — an ssh payload rather than a keystroke.
   *
   * **A generator, where the original answers a `Typed[]` of the same length as the input.** A 4 KB
   * `CHANNEL_DATA` payload allocates 4,096 records there, of which all but a handful are
   * `Typed.nothing()` — two empty arrays each. Here the caller steps it and the ones it has finished
   * with are gone.
   *
   * Also the first **synchronous** generator in this directory. Every other one is an `async gen`
   * over a capability, and this one suspends against nothing: the bytes are all in hand and the
   * laziness is only about not building the list. Worth saying because it is the case that shows
   * `gen` is not a coroutine-over-IO feature — `../../../QUESTIONS.md` frames stepping in terms of
   * awaiting throughout, and this needs the stepping without the awaiting.
   */
  gen<Typed> void feedAll(this, Bytes bytes) {
    for (i32 i = 0; i < bytes.len(); i++) { yield this.feed(bytes.get(i)); }
  }

  /**
   * A byte at a time, nothing edited and nothing drawn — the non-canonical half.
   *
   * None of the editing characters mean anything: `^U`, `^W` and DEL are bytes, and so is `^D`, so a
   * program in this mode has no end of input from the terminal — that is the mode rather than an
   * omission. `^C` and `^\` still signal, because `ISIG` is a third flag and nothing here clears it.
   */
  Typed feedByte(this, u8 b) {
    Bytes drawn = this.mode.echo ? glyph(b) : Bytes.empty();
    if (b == INTR) { return Typed(drawn, Signal(Interrupt)); }
    if (b == QUIT) { return Typed(drawn, Signal(Quit)); }
    return Typed(drawn, Delivered(one(b)));
  }

  /** The canonical rules, unchanged from the original but for the shape of what they answer. */
  Typed feedLine(this, u8 b) {
    if (this.literal) {
      this.literal = false;
      this.held.push(b);
      // The `^` from the `^V` is on the screen already; back over it and show what arrived.
      Buf out = Buf.create();
      out.push(8);
      out.pushAll(glyph(b));
      return Typed.echoed(out.bytes());
    }
    if (b == LNEXT) {
      this.literal = true;
      return Typed.echoed(glyph('^'));
    }
    if (b == 13 || b == 10) {
      // ICRNL: a carriage return *is* a newline to the program, and the echo is a full CRLF because
      // a terminal's cursor does not return on its own.
      Buf line = Buf.create();
      line.pushAll(this.held.bytes());
      line.push(10);
      this.held = Buf.create();
      return Typed(crlf(), Delivered(line.bytes()));
    }
    if (b == ERASE) {
      if (this.held.len == 0) { return Typed.nothing(); }
      // **The columns the character occupied, not one.** A control character is shown as `^A`, so
      // erasing it takes two backspaces — and `columns` is `glyph(…).len()`, so the two cannot
      // disagree the way they did.
      i32 wide = columns(this.held.get(this.held.len - 1));
      this.held.truncate(this.held.len - 1);
      return Typed.echoed(rubOut(wide));
    }
    if (b == KILL) {
      i32 n = this.widthFrom(0);
      this.held = Buf.create();
      return Typed.echoed(rubOut(n));
    }
    if (b == WERASE) {
      // **Bytes to drop and columns to rub out are two numbers**, and they differ the moment a word
      // holds a control character: `^A` is one byte and two columns.
      i32 from = this.wordStart();
      i32 wide = this.widthFrom(from);
      this.held.truncate(from);
      return Typed.echoed(rubOut(wide));
    }
    if (b == INTR) {
      this.held = Buf.create();
      // `^C` and no newline: what follows it belongs to whoever is interrupted.
      return Typed("^C".toBytes(), Signal(Interrupt));
    }
    if (b == QUIT) {
      this.held = Buf.create();
      // The line goes, exactly as `^C`'s does — measured through the pty, and the rule nothing had
      // asked about until `^\` was tried.
      return Typed("^\\".toBytes(), Signal(Quit));
    }
    if (b == REPRINT) {
      // `^R` echoes itself, breaks the line, and draws what has been typed again.
      Buf out = Buf.create();
      out.pushAll("^R".toBytes());
      out.pushAll(crlf());
      for (i32 i = 0; i < this.held.len; i++) { out.pushAll(glyph(this.held.get(i))); }
      return Typed.echoed(out.bytes());
    }
    if (b == EOFC) {
      if (this.held.len == 0) { return Typed(Bytes.empty(), Eof); }
      // A line without a newline on it. The program sees exactly what was typed, and the terminal
      // shows nothing, because nothing was typed *at* the terminal.
      Bytes got = this.held.bytes();
      this.held = Buf.create();
      return Typed(Bytes.empty(), Delivered(got));
    }
    this.held.push(b);
    return Typed.echoed(glyph(b));
  }

  /**
   * Where `^W` erases back to: past whatever is not a word character, and then past the word.
   *
   * Two passes, and the first is why `abc def^W^W` takes back `def` and then `abc ` rather than
   * stopping at the space it just uncovered.
   */
  i32 wordStart(const this) {
    Bytes all = this.held.bytes();
    i32 at = all.len();
    while (at > 0 && !isWordChar(all.get(at - 1))) { at = at - 1; }
    while (at > 0 && isWordChar(all.get(at - 1))) { at = at - 1; }
    return at;
  }

  /** The columns occupied from byte `from` to the end of the line. */
  i32 widthFrom(const this, i32 from) {
    Bytes all = this.held.bytes();
    i32 w = 0;
    for (i32 i = from; i < all.len(); i++) { w = w + columns(all.get(i)); }
    return w;
  }
}

/**
 * Whether this byte is part of a word, for `^W`.
 *
 * Letters and digits and nothing else, which is what the kernel does: a dot, a slash, a tab and a
 * control character all end a word, so `ls /usr/bin^W` takes back `bin`. A byte over 127 is not a
 * word character either — a UTF-8 letter arrives as two or three of those, and treating them as one
 * is a question about characters that a *byte* discipline does not get to answer.
 */
bool isWordChar(u8 b) {
  return (b >= '0' && b <= '9') || (b >= 'A' && b <= 'Z') || (b >= 'a' && b <= 'z');
}

Bytes one(u8 b) {
  Buf out = Buf.create();
  out.push(b);
  return out.bytes();
}

// ## What could not be written
//
// **`Buf` cannot drop a byte off the end, and that is an `O(n²)` word erase in the shipped file.**
// `packages/bytes/src/buf.wac` has `push`, `pushAll`, `dropFront`, `take` and `bytes`, and nothing
// that shortens from the back — so `line.wac`'s `dropLast` takes the whole buffer, copies all but
// the last byte into a new one, and replaces it. One full copy per backspace, and `^W` calls it once
// per byte of the word: erasing a ten-character word at the end of a 4,000-byte line copies about
// 40,000 bytes to remove ten.
//
// `this.held.truncate(n)` is written above and does not exist. It is four lines — the array stays,
// `len` moves — and `dropFront` is the same operation from the other end, already there. Filed
// rather than added, because `packages/bytes` is under everything and the gate has had no memory to
// run in all day. This file is the consumer that wants it.
//
// **Nothing can ask for a mode.** `Mode` is a value here and `setMode` swaps it, which is half the
// answer: the other half is that a *program* running under a terminal has no way to say *give me a
// keystroke at a time*. `sshd` holds the `Line`, the program holds an `In`, and there is nothing
// between them. In `vision/std` that is one member —
//
//     Result<Mode, NotATerminal> setMode(Mode m);   // on `In`, answering the mode it was in
//
// — and it is the first capability in this exercise that has to answer *what it was*, because
// restoring is the caller's job and guessing the previous mode is wrong for anyone who was in a
// password prompt. Which makes it also the first that wants `defer` for a reason nothing else has:
// not to release something acquired, but to put something back.
//
// **And the mode belongs to the terminal, not to the reader.** Two programs sharing one terminal —
// a shell and the editor it spawned — hold two `In` values over one discipline, and a mode set
// through either is visible to both. That is what a terminal *is*, and it is the one piece of
// ambient state a capability system cannot make private without making it wrong. Worth naming as
// the exception rather than fixing: `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
